      "a": "Summary\nSummary: HTTPS deploy. Same as the backend lab. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Walk the snippet like this: `fetch(\"https:` — ml.example.com/predict\", { method: \"POST\", body, headers }). A line you can point at: fetch(\"https://ml.example.com/predict\", { method: \"POST\", body, headers }) In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. Passwords, cookies, and tokens are not postcards. Interviewers want TLS named, not 'the green lock magic'. Never post passwords to http://. An https page calling http is mixed content. Putting a key in frontend code is not fixed by HTTPS. Name Create/Read/Update/Delete if this is a list, or 401 vs 403 if this is login.\n\nThe problem before\nLogin rode http:// on cafe Wi-Fi. Anyone could read the password. Mixed content blocked https pages that still called http APIs. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. Same as the backend lab. Never post passwords to http://. An https page calling http is mixed content. Putting a key in frontend code is not fixed by HTTPS.\n\nWhat this is\nSame as the backend lab. HTTPS is HTTP inside TLS. The padlock encrypts the pipe and checks the certificate. The verbs are still GET and POST. Redirect http to https at the edge. Walk the snippet like this: `fetch(\"https:` — ml.example.com/predict\", { method: \"POST\", body, headers }). Passwords, cookies, and tokens are not postcards. Interviewers want TLS named, not 'the green lock magic'. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nWhat it solves\nPasswords, cookies, and tokens are not postcards. Interviewers want TLS named, not 'the green lock magic'. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). Same as the backend lab. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA sealed envelope vs a postcard. Same letter inside. The street cannot read the envelope. The address on the outside is still HTTP's URL. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. HTTPS deploy. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nUses\nEvery login, payment, cookie, fetch to an API. Localhost labs may skip it; production must not. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: HTTPS deploy. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. Passwords, cookies, and tokens are not postcards. Interviewers want TLS named, not 'the green lock magic'.\n\nWatch out\nNever post passwords to http://. An https page calling http is mixed content. Putting a key in frontend code is not fixed by HTTPS. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. Same as the backend lab.",
