      "q": "Practice: HTTPS + key from the notebook",
      "a": "Summary\nSummary: HTTPS + key from the notebook. Never hardcode the key in a public gist. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Read the snippet under this question. The green comments on the right say what each line is doing. A line you can point at: key = os.environ[\"API_KEY\"] In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. Passwords, cookies, and tokens are not postcards. Interviewers want TLS named, not 'the green lock magic'. Never post passwords to http://. An https page calling http is mixed content. Putting a key in frontend code is not fixed by HTTPS.\n\nThe problem before\nLogin rode http:// on cafe Wi-Fi. Anyone could read the password. Mixed content blocked https pages that still called http APIs. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. Never hardcode the key in a public gist. Never post passwords to http://. An https page calling http is mixed content. Putting a key in frontend code is not fixed by HTTPS.\n\nWhat this is\nNever hardcode the key in a public gist. HTTPS is HTTP inside TLS. The padlock encrypts the pipe and checks the certificate. The verbs are still GET and POST. Redirect http to https at the edge. Read the snippet under this question. The green comments on the right say what each line is doing. Passwords, cookies, and tokens are not postcards. Interviewers want TLS named, not 'the green lock magic'.\n\nWhat it solves\nPasswords, cookies, and tokens are not postcards. Interviewers want TLS named, not 'the green lock magic'. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). Never hardcode the key in a public gist. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA sealed envelope vs a postcard. Same letter inside. The street cannot read the envelope. The address on the outside is still HTTP's URL. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. HTTPS + key from the notebook. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nUses\nEvery login, payment, cookie, fetch to an API. Localhost labs may skip it; production must not. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: HTTPS + key from the notebook. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. Passwords, cookies, and tokens are not postcards. Interviewers want TLS named, not 'the green lock magic'.\n\nWatch out\nNever post passwords to http://. An https page calling http is mixed content. Putting a key in frontend code is not fixed by HTTPS. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. Never hardcode the key in a public gist.",
