      "a": "Summary\nSummary: Spam vs ham labels. 0/1 or spam/ham. Keep a codebook. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Read the snippet under this question. The green comments on the right say what each line is doing. A line you can point at: y = [0, 0, 1, 1]  # 1 = spam In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. You get a working slice you can explain in an interview: input, check, change, result. Not a 200-file repo. Do not skip validation. Do not leave secrets in the snippet. If the lab is client-only, say that the real server must still check.\n\nThe problem before\nThe feature was only a comment in someone's head. The page looked done until a refresh, a second user, or a wrong id. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. 0/1 or spam/ham. Keep a codebook. Do not skip validation. Do not leave secrets in the snippet. If the lab is client-only, say that the real server must still check.\n\nWhat this is\n0/1 or spam/ham. Keep a codebook. This lab is one small complete job. Read the function name, then each comment. Copy it, run it, hide it, write it again. That is how the sheet is built. Read the snippet under this question. The green comments on the right say what each line is doing. You get a working slice you can explain in an interview: input, check, change, result. Not a 200-file repo.\n\nWhat it solves\nYou get a working slice you can explain in an interview: input, check, change, result. Not a 200-file repo. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). 0/1 or spam/ham. Keep a codebook. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA cooking class card: one dish, one method. You do not open five cookbooks. You follow this card, then the next. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet. Spam vs ham labels. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nUses\nEvery practice row on this page. Same pattern in frontend, API, SQL, and cloud. On this sheet, the question is: Spam vs ham labels. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. You get a working slice you can explain in an interview: input, check, change, result. Not a 200-file repo.\n\nWatch out\nDo not skip validation. Do not leave secrets in the snippet. If the lab is client-only, say that the real server must still check. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. 0/1 or spam/ham. Keep a codebook.",
      "code": "y = [0, 0, 1, 1]  # 1 = spam"
